{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 claims that 'distinct failure mode signatures (different error types, timing) under similar stresses' indicate 'different causal paths' and thereby evidence of true independence. But distinct error signatures are fully counterexampled by common-cause failures with heterogeneous downstream effects: a single shared cause (e.g., a leap-second bug, a config push affecting mixed stacks, or a common TLS library) routinely produces different error types and timing across nodes depending on local state, load, and retry policy. Thus different signatures are compatible with a shared root cause and do not distinguish true independence from apparent uncorrelation — the exact distinction the problem demands.","counterexample":[{"shared_cause":"A single leap-second event causes NTP-based time jumps across independently operated services.","distinct_signatures":"Service A throws timeout exceptions with monotonic jitter; Service B corrupts timestamps in its write-ahead log; Service C rejects certificates with apparent expiry errors.","same_root":"All three are caused by the identical shared timekeeping fault, yet the error types, timing, and affected components differ — matching SRC_001's proposed 'evidence for different causal paths' while being causally dependent."}]},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 frames the comparison as conditional (decentralized is more resilient 'only when' a demonstrable causal independence barrier exists) and defers to 'the number of services N is a red herring' in favor of causal-graph diversity. But the problem asks to argue for or against the specific claim comparing a decentralized network of small services to ONE LARGE SERVICE with strictly more total internal redundancy. By sidelining the total-internal-redundancy dimension, SRC_002 avoids the hardest adversarial case: a monolith whose internal redundancy is heterogeneous across causally-isolated failure domains. Its evidence standard (separate supply chains, codebases, teams, jurisdictions) is a sufficient condition for independence but is not the only way a single large service can achieve it, so it does not address whether the large redundant service could instantiate the same independence barrier internally.","counterexample":[{"architecture":"A single large service internally partitioned into k isolated cells with disjoint runtime stacks, teams, and deployments, and with k-fold internal redundancy distributed across cells.","property":"The large service has strictly more total internal redundancy than any single cell, yet it meets SRC_002's causal-independence barrier (distinct codebases, teams, supply chains within the monolith boundary).","implication":"Under SRC_002's own criterion, the monolith qualifies as resilient, so the criterion cannot by itself establish that the decentralized network is MORE resilient than the internally-redundant large service."}]}]}